<section id="mathml-demo">
  <h2>Extra Credit: MathML</h2>
  <p>
    Here's Einstein's mass&ndash;energy equivalence formula, written with MathML instead of an image:
  </p>
  <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
    <mi>E</mi>
    <mo>=</mo>
    <mi>m</mi>
    <msup>
      <mi>c</mi>
      <mn>2</mn>
    </msup>
  </math>
  <p>
    MathML has technically been part of the HTML spec for a long time, but for years it wasn't something
    developers could actually rely on. According to <a href="https://caniuse.com/mathml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">caniuse.com</a>,
    Chrome (and Chromium-based browsers like Edge) didn't ship built-in MathML support until relatively
    recently. For a long stretch, Chrome either had no support or required a polyfill like MathJax,
    even though Firefox and Safari had supported it for years. That's a big deal because Chrome/Chromium
    has such a large browser market share: a spec feature is only really <em>usable</em> in production
    once it works consistently across the browsers most of your users actually have.
  </p>
</section>
